# Shell scripts must keep LF endings whatever a contributor's core.autocrlf is set
# to. A CRLF shebang is not a portability nuisance on Unix - it is fatal: the kernel
# reads the interpreter as "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and the script dies with a bare
# "bad interpreter" naming a binary that visibly exists.
#
# The .ps1/.bat side is deliberately left alone: those files are LF in the tree today
# and PowerShell reads them fine, so pinning them to CRLF would rewrite every one of
# them for no gain.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
